326-## 10. Release-Gating Logic
327-
328-Possible gates:
329:- release gate
330-- rollout gate
331-- canary continuation gate
332-- human-review gate
333-- compliance gate
334-- prompt-version promotion gate
335-- semantic-drift gate
336-- variant-consistency gate
337-- control-loop packet parity gate
338-- guide-vs-runtime lookup parity gate
339-- skill-layer packet parity gate
340-- quality-iteration continuation gate
341-- adaptation-promotion gate
342-- benchmark-registry gate
343-- context-sufficiency gate
344-- critique-quality gate
345-- route-quality gate
346-- coding-proof gate
347-- benchmark-execution gate
348-- replay-verdict gate
349-- adaptation-lifecycle gate
350:- telemetry-trend gate
351-- lifecycle-fidelity gate
352-- packet-compliance gate
353-- behavior-replay gate
354-- delegation-join gate
355-- approval-lifecycle gate
356-- release-evidence gate
357-
358-Threshold rules:
359-- define thresholds only where action follows
360:- safety and compliance thresholds should be stricter than stylistic ones
361-- noisy metrics require stronger evidence before disruptive rollback
362-- when monitoring is iterative or continuous, define stagnation thresholds and intervention actions, not only quality thresholds
363-- when route choice changes across checkpoints, define continuation, fallback, and stop actions explicitly
364-
365-A gate is not real unless it has:
366-- owner
367-- threshold
368-- action
369-
370-### 10.1 Candidate labeling and gate integrity
371-
372-For prompt, workflow, or release candidates, preserve at least:
373-- baseline identity
374-- candidate identity
375-- cohort or environment boundary
376-- gate owner
377-- pass / hold / reject outcome
378-
379-Gate-integrity rule:
380-- if candidate identity or cohort labeling is ambiguous, narrow the conclusion before escalating the gate
381-
382-### 10.2 Iteration-gated continuation
383-
384-When the gate is mid-execution rather than final:
385-- preserve the current checkpoint state
386-- preserve the baseline or prior accepted state
387-- preserve the next route and fallback route
388-- preserve the stop trigger
389-- do not promote repeated improvement into a persistent default without an explicit adaptation decision
390-
--
441-Rules:
442-- use explicit criteria
443-- keep anchors visible
444-- separate factual scoring from style preference
445-- prefer comparative judging when absolute scales are unstable
446:- never treat a single judge score as unquestionable truth
447:- do not let judge-style scoring replace obvious deterministic harness checks
448-
449-## 12. Reporting Contract
450-
451-Unless the user requests another format, prefer:
452-
453-### Acknowledgment
454-- restate the evaluation scope and compared systems
455-
456-### Analysis
457-- define the evaluation surface, criteria, and relevant thresholds
458-
459-### Execution
460-- present the scorecard, findings, or comparison result
461-- identify regressions, strengths, blockers, and recommendation
462-
463-### Impact & Risk
464-- state release sensitivity, unresolved risks, drift signals, or anomaly concerns
465-
466-### Verification
467-- state what evidence the evaluation is based on
468-- state what remains uncertain or weakly supported
469-- state the safest next step: approve, hold, re-test, narrow rollout, or reject
470-
471-### 12.1 Evaluation close-out rule
472-
473-A good Codex-facing eval close-out usually keeps:
474-- compared candidates or surfaces
475-- decisive regressions or passes
476-- gate outcome and owner-relevant next step
477-- one explicit uncertainty if instrumentation or cohorts were weak
--
481-Avoid:
482-- measuring the wrong surface
